## 1. Introduction
Over the last two decades, numerous advanced porous materials (APMs) – encompassing metal–organic frameworks (MOFs),[1,2] covalent organic frameworks (COFs),[3.4] porous organic polymers (POPs),[5-7] supramolecular frameworks,[8,9] zeolites,[10] and others[11] – have been developed and explored in many fields of science and technology. Particularly, MOFs and COFs are notable for their ability to extend the precision of molecular chemistry to extended frameworks.[12] MOFs are constructed from metal-oxide units joined by organic linkers through metal–ligand coordination bonds.[13] Although MOFs provide an extensive combination of metal ions and organic linkers to achieve desired structures and functionalities, the relative strength of the coordination bond often restricts their practical implementation, especially under very harsh conditions.[14] To address these drawbacks, covalent organic frameworks (COFs) have been proposed as a wholly organic alternative architecture to MOFs.[3] Whilst sharing high crystallinity, porosity, and tuneability with their MOF counterparts,[15-19] COFs further exhibit highly stable structures against harsh chemical environments including boiling water, strong acids and bases, and oxidation and reduction conditions.[20,21] This advantage has attracted substantial research interest, with hopes of not only complementing, but also surpassing the development of other well-known crystalline porous materials such as MOFs and zeolites.
In general, COFs are made entirely from light elements (typically H, B, C, N, O, Si),[22] which poses some intrinsic limitations on their physical and chemical properties. For example, the relatively lower density and weaker interlayer molecular forces lead to the loose packing in the preparation of 2D COF membranes or thin films.[23] Furthermore, the resulting higher hydrophobicity do not match the surface compatibility for materials chemistry and biocompatibility for biologic systems. More critically, the metal-free COFs generally exhibit unsophisticated functionalities which cannot meet the requirements of modern science. In contrast, recent applicationsled development of solid porous materials in the areas of gas adsorption and separation,[24] heterogeneous catalysis,[25,26] electrochemical energy storage,[27] etc, have emphasized the role of metals in their structures. The targeted introduction of metal ions into porous COFs to form metal-covalent organic frameworks (MCOFs) may be instrumental in addressing these shortcomings (Figure 1). In a sense, MCOFs can establish a bridge between MOFs and COFs which exhibit a balanced mix of crystallinity, porosity, stability, and tunability, leading to self-complementary features between these two types of materials. Currently, it is indisputable that MCOFs have become an interdisciplinary subject between polymer chemistry and inorganic chemistry. However, the development of MCOFs is still in its infancy and remains a challenge for the years to come. In this Minireview, for the first time, we systematically summarize the recent progress of MCOFs regarding the design strategy, synthesis, structural characterization, and their cutting-edge applications. We believe that offering a timely critical review on this topic toward a wide range of readers is of great importance for the future design and development of functional COFs.
## 2. Synthesis and characterization of MCOFs
## 2.1. Synthetic strategies
As shown in Figure 2, the synthesis of MCOFs, in principle, can be classified either into direct synthesis or post-synthetic metalation. Generally, employing metal-coordinating molecules or complexes as building blocks is a straightforward approach to construct MCOFs (Figure 2a). Indeed, most of reported MCOFs have been extensively developed by direct synthetic strategy using prevalent metal complexation. The main attractiveness of this approach is the utilization of abundant metal precursors for expanding the functionalities of COFs. However, very often, some potential issues of metal precursors, such as the sensitivity, stability, solubility, and compatibility, limit the synthesis of MCOFs.[28] To address this issue and simplify the synthesis, subcomponent self-assembly combining dynamic covalent (e.g., imine bonds) and coordinative bonds can be employed for the fabrication of MCOFs (Figure 2b). This strategy has been widely used to construct a library of various supramolecular coordination cages.[29] Notably, this approach generally requires a dynamic and reversible feature during the bond formation which leads to the possibility of metal-to-metal exchange in the frameworks.[30] Moreover, the metal ion-driven assembly may further improve the crystallization behaviors of MCOFs. For situations where the targeted MCOFs cannot be achieved through direct synthesis due to the different reactivity of metal precursors, post-synthetic metalation is an alternative approach to decorate targeted metal ions into the frameworks by metal-ligand coordination,[31] thus providing a reliable way to produce MCOFs for specific applications (Figure 2c). As for the characterization of MCOFs, their crystalline structures are generally determined by the combination of refinement of powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) data, pore size distribution analysis, Transmission electron microscope (TEM) and modelling studies. In very few cases, the structures of MCOFs can be solved by single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD),[32] which can observe the precise location of metals in the framework. To highlight the metals in the COFs, we will term all the materials as “M-COF” (M represent the different metals) to replace their name in the literatures. It is helpful to divide the synthetic discussion to focus on two-dimensional (2D) and three-dimensional (3D) structures.
Generally, 2D MCOFs are constructed by using planar building blocks to form stacked layered structures.[4] Several popular building blocks can be considered for the development of 2D MCOFs. In this section, we highlight key monomer classes for MCOF synthesis.
Firstly, salen ligands are well suited to coordinate various metal ions to form tetradentate M- ${\bf\cdot N}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ or pentadentate M- $N_{2}O_{2}X$ The resulting M(salen) species have been extensively used to develop the most powerful homogeneous catalysts,[33] thereby offering an opportunity to synthesize 2D MCOFs. For example, a series of 2D MCOFs are obtained by metalation of a highly stable salen-based COF with different metal ions including ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{N i}}^{2+}$ , $Z n^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}$ , and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ , endowing them as promising heterogeneous catalysts (Figure 3a).[34] On the other hand, it should be noted that a wide variety of highly selective asymmetric reactions have been disclosed by using chiral M(salen) complexes.[33] Inspired by such privileged chiral ligands, our group employed the subcomponent self-assembly to synthesize 2D chiral Zn-COFs consisting of Zn(salen) moieties as heterogeneous chiral catalysts (Figure 3b-d).[30] Specially, the ZnCOF is linked by the cooperative action of coordination and covalent bonds simultaneously. Unusually, the $Z n^{2+}$ in the open frameworks can be exchanged by other metal ions such as ${\mathsf{C r}}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}$ , and $\mathsf{V}^{4+}$ due to the relative liability of $z_{n-\mathrm{O}/\mathsf{N}}$ bonds. Notably, other 2D MCOF systems cannot exhibit such metal-to-metal exchange behavior, highlighting the structural uniqueness of M(salen)-based COFs. However, it is difficult to determine the precise location of the exchanged metal ions in the structures. Our work demonstrates the first example of metal-tometal exchange in MCOFs, which could facilitate the development of multivariate MCOFs for cooperative interactions in heterogeneous catalyst.
Secondly, porphyrins are naturally occurring macrocyclic compounds that can be utilized for cooperative coordination of various metal ions to form four-coordinated, five-coordinated, or six-coordinated complexes.[35] The resulting metalloporphyrin complexes have been used to develop multifunctional materials due to their advantageous properties of strong light absorption, high emission, and rich coordination chemistry.[36] In view of their highly symmetric and planar molecular geometry, as well as unprecedented coordination properties. Metalloporphyrins provide a good opportunity to build 2D MCOFs with multifunctionalities. For example, 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4- aminophenyl)porphinato]cobalt [Co(TAP)] can be used to produce a 2D Co-COF with AA stacking (Figure 4a), and the channel width of this Co-COF can be tuned from 21 to $24\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ by replacing 1,4-benzenedicarboxaldehyde (BDA) with biphenyl4,4'-dicarboxaldehyde (BPDA) (Figure 4b).[37] Moreover, metalloporphyrin-based 2D MCOFs could be a rich resource for the development of novel ultrathin 2D nanomaterials, which is deserving of study due to the more accessible metal sites exposed on the surface, possibly increasing the functional performance. One example showed that a 2D Co-COF containing well-organized Tetrathiafulvalene (TTF) and Co-porphyrin units can be exfoliated into ultrathin 2D nanosheets (5 - 6 nm thickness) with enhanced $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ conversion (Figure 4c).[38] However, the largescale production of high-quality 2D MCOF nanosheets is still a challenge. In this aspect, Jiang and co-workers developed a scalable bottom-up approach to synthesize few-layered nanosheets ( $1.1-1.3\ n m$ thickness) of 2D Co-COF by iminecondensation of CoTAP and BDA or BPDA (Figure 4d-g).[39] The key step is to add a large excess amount of 2,4,6- trimethylbenzaldehyde as inhibitors in the reaction, which could not only hinder the axial $\uppi{-}\uppi$ stacking but also allow the anisotropic growth of the Co-COF along the 2D directions. Note that the most attractiveness of this approach is the high-yield production of the Co-COF nanosheets $(55\%-58\%)$ , exhibiting much higher than the top-down liquid exfoliation (lower than $10\%$ ).
Thirdly, although many 2D COFs are fully π-conjugated layered structures, they generally possess limited conductivity that severely restricts the cutting-edge applications such as optoelectronic devices. The sagacious choice of organic building blocks featuring high charge carrier mobility is crucial for the development of conductive 2D MCOFs. Metallophthalocyanines are highly planar and large π-electronic macrocycles with broad absorption profiles that could serve as building units to develop 2D MCOFs with high intrinsic conductivity.[40] For example, two 2D MCOFs $\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ or $Z n^{2+}$ ) were synthesized by condensation of tert-butyl pyrene-tetraone (tBu-PT) and Cu- or Znphthalocyanine (Figure 5a).[41] Both materials exhibited a p-type semiconductor with a band gap of $\sim1.2\mathsf{e V}$ and conductivity of ∼ $5\times10^{-7}~\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{c m}$ . However, the conductivity of both 2D MCOFs is moderate. One of the possible reasons is due to the serrated AA stacking model caused by the steric hindrance of tert-butyl groups (Figure 5c and 5e), which greatly affected the charge carrier transport along the out-of-plane direction. Mirica and co-workers removed the tert-butyl groups from ${}^{t}\mathsf{B u}\mathsf{-P T}$ to obtain a highly planar linker, which can be used to synthesize a 2D Ni-COF with eclipsed AA stacking by the condensation with Ni-phthalocyanine (Figure 5b).[42] Note that such perfect AA stacking would endow the possibility for significant contribution to the intrinsic bulk conductivity from the out-of-plane charge-transport (Figure 5d and 5f). As a result, this 2D Ni-COF displayed a high conductivity with $2.51\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S/m}$ . Both studies examine the influence of interlayer stacking on the conductivities of 2D MCOFs.
