Finally, 2,2'-bipyridine (bpy) is a $C_{2}$ -symmetric and planar molecule and represents one of the most often used chelating ligands for forming metal complexes in organometallic chemistry. Its derivatives have been employed as popular building blocks for the development of 2D MCOFs through post-synthetic metalation. For example, a Re-COF with AA stacking was obtained by metalation of a 2,2'-bipyridine-based COF with $\mathsf{R e}(\mathsf{C O})_{5}\mathsf{C l}$ (Figure 6a).[43] The reliable structural information of Re coordination environment has been measured by $\mathsf{X}$ -ray absorption spectroscopy, indicating the $\mathsf{R e}(\mathsf{b p y})(\mathsf{C O})_{3}\mathsf{C l}$ is well preserved in the structure. Another case demonstrated that the lanthanide ions (Ln) can be successfully coordinated into a 2,2-bipyridyl-derived COF for the development of 2D Ln-COFs (Figure 6b).[44] Note that the introduction of lanthanide ions could significantly expand the luminescent properties for the COFs due to their well-separated peaks and near-infrared (NIR) emission. However, most 2,2- bipyridyl-based MCOFs adopt eclipsed AA-stacking model and generally have larger mesopores, which may limit their application in gas separation and catalysis. To address this challenge, our group developed an approach to control the pore size of 2,2- bipyridyl-derived 2D Ir-COFs by tuning the interlayer steric hindrance (Figure 6c-d).[45] The addition of alkyl groups in the 2D frameworks leads to the transformation of interlayer stacking from AA to ABC stacking, as well as transformation of the hexagonal mesopore to triangular micropore with more exposed surface within the channels (Figure 6e-f), which have been further demonstrated the advantage for heterogenous catalysis.
## 2.3. Synthesis and characterization of 3D MCOFs
Compared to 2D MCOFs, 3D MCOFs could characteristically endow numerous open metal sites with various orientation and show promising applications in different areas, especially for catalysis and gas separation.[4] Obviously, their structures are more complicated but intriguing due to possible interpenetration of the frameworks, thus making it difficult to predict and reveal their exact crystal structures. A proper choice of building blocks with correct geometric shapes is necessary to yield crystalline 3D frameworks. Tetraphenylmethane and its derivatives possess a perfect tetrahedral molecular geometry, making the possiblity for the development of 3D MCOFs. For example, a 3D Cu-COF can be obtained through $[4+4]$ imine-condensation, which crystallizes in $P m c2_{1}$ space group and adopts a 2-fold interpenetrated pts topology (Figure 7a).[46] In addition, a 3D Ni-COF was achieved through $\ [4\ +\ 3]$ condensation followed by metalation with $N i(C O D)_{2}$ , which adopts a non-interpenetrated 3D cubic lattice with a bor net topology (Figure 7b).[47] Our group recently synthesized two 3D Zn-COFs containing Zn(salen) moieties through $[4+2]$ imine-condensation followed by metalation with $Z n(O A C)_{2}$ , which showed a 7-fold interpenetrated diamondoid open framework (Figure 7c-e).[48] Notably, both Zn-COFs can be also obtained by subcomponent self-assembly approach. Taken together, the above three cases indicate that tetraphenylmethane derivatives are intriguing building blocks for development of 3D MCOFs.
The decrease of molecular symmetry from tetrahedral to distorted tetrahedral shape also leads to 3D MCOFs with interesting topologies. In 2016, Yaghi group reported the first example of 3D MCOFs with woven structures.[49] In this case a distorted tetrahedral complex $[\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{P D B})_{2}(\mathsf{B F}_{4})]$ was employed to synthesize a 3D Cu-COF (Figure 8a). The experimentally obtained crystal structure shows that the $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ centres are topologically independent of the weaving within the Cu-COF structure and serve as templates for bringing the threads into a woven pattern (Figure 8b), thus forming a 2-fold interpenetrated 3D framework with an underlying dia topology. However, the interpenetrated structure of this Cu-COF limits the available internal space and restricts the dynamic motion of the threads. To avoid this issue, an anion exchange strategy was employed to overcome the structural interpenetration when forming the woven structure. For example, the exchange of $\mathsf{B F}_{4}^{-}$ by a bulky anion $(P O_{2}P h_{2}^{-}$ , diphenylphosphinate) enables the occupation of the internal voids in the framework (Figure 8c-d).[50] This new isoreticular 3D Cu-COF has been demonstrated to be a noninterpenetrated woven structure, thus giving credence to the notion of a novel mode of motional dynamics in solids as termed ‘adaptive inclusion’. This study provides an example of how to avoid structural interpenetration in 3D M-COFs.
Although the above strategy can obtain 3D M-COFs with non-interpenetrated woven structure, it is still difficult to control their crystal sizes with homogeneous distribution, especially obtaining uniform nanocrystals. This aspect is actually crucial to prepare high-quality membranes, thin films and devices. The key factor is how to control their nucleation and crystal growth for correcting defects during the condensation reactions. The utilization of Boc group protected amine building block $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N H B o c}\cdot\mathsf{D I P})_{2}(\mathsf{B F}_{4})_{2}$ allowed generation of the 3D Co-COF through a homogeneous synthetic route (Figure 9a-c).[51] This method enabled the isolation of uniform nanocrystals $(\sim200\mathsf{n m})$ of 3D MCOFs with the desired structure (Figure 9d).
