## 3. Emerging applications
## 3.1. Catalysis
MCOFs are a structurally, compositionally varied class of highly stable porous materials. The spatial confinement of substrates within the pores can induce specific host-guest interactions with the MCOF skeletons so that it promotes the catalytic reactions with high efficiency. Compared with homogenous catalysts, MCOFs generally display several advantages: (i) The pore sizes of MCOFs can be finely tuned by the building blocks, leading to a confined environment with size-selective catalysis. (ii) The metal ions are anchored in the pore walls which could prevent their aggregation without loss of catalytic performance. (iii) Their structures are highly stable against harsh chemical systems including boiling water, strong acids and bases. (iv) They are insoluble in solvents and can be easily separated from the catalytic reactions for recyclable uses. In light of a broad structural diversity, MCOFs enable the integration of various metal sites into the structures and are tolerant to different types of catalytic reactions under various chemical systems.
## 3.1.1 Organic chemical transformations
Incorporating noble metals (such as Pd and Ir) into highly porous COFs could give rise to powerful heterogeneous catalysts for organic chemical transformations. For example, a 3D Pd-COF exhibited excellent photocatalytic performance for selective oxidation of thioanisole to methyl phenyl sulfoxide with $98\%$ yield, which was comparable to many reported heterogeneous photocatalysts.[52] Interestingly, in this case the 3D Pd-COF showed much higher photocatalytic performance than the 2D PdCOF with the same Pd-porphyrin units (Figure 10a), which may be due to the alignments of Pd-porphyrins are almost hidden in 2D Pd-COF by the face-to-face AA stacking. This work highlights the orientation of metal sites in MCOFs greatly influence the catalytic activity. Furthermore, tuning the pore size of MCOFs with more accessible metal sites would be pretty important for catalysis. In 2018, our group demonstrated that the Ir-COFs-iPr and Ir-COFs-Et with ABC stacking exhibited better catalytic performance than AA stacking-based Ir-COFs-H for dehydrogenative borylation of aromatic $C-H$ bonds (Figure 10b).[45] Two plausible reasons could explain the catalytic activities with the order Ir-COF-iPr > Ir-COF-Et $>$ Ir-COF-H: (i) The ABC stacking model could convert the larger hexagonal mesopores to smaller triangular micropores with suitable pore widths and more exposed metal sites. (ii) High crystallinity of IrCOFs is also beneficial for the catalytic performances. This work suggests that the suitable pore size, accessible metal sites, high crystallinity and structural stability are crucial to the catalytic activity of MCOFs.
Because of the discrepancy between asymmetry and crystallinity in chiral MCOFs, it is essential to understand the chiral expression from the molecules to their assembled frameworks. For example, a group of chiral 2D MCOFs obtained from metalto-metal exchange of a Zn-COF exhibited excellent activity and enantioselectivity for several catalytic reactions (Figure 11).[30] The combination of Mn and Cr centers was shown to be effective in a sequential asymmetric reaction involving alkene epoxidation and epoxide ring-opening. Compared to the monometallic MnCOF and Cr-COF, which catalyzed the individual steps with 77 - $97\%$ ee and $82\cdot96\%$ ee (Figure 11), the bimetallic Mn-Cr-COF was able to catalyze the two-step reaction with up to $91\%$ ee at just 1 mol $\%$ loading. This approach avoids the need to separate intermediates, minimizing the number of steps. The combination of high crystallinity, porosity, stability, and unhindered chirality render those chiral MCOFs as powerful asymmetric heterogeneous catalysts.
## 3.1.2 CO2 reduction
The efficient conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into high value-added products such as CO, MeOH, EtOH, $C H_{4}$ has become an important subject relevant to carbon emission and energy resources. So far, the traditional catalysts used for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ conversion mainly rely on metals, metal dichalcogenides, or metal oxides etc. However, they still suffer some issues, such as the ability of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption and capture, the efficiency of intermolecular electron transmission, and the reaction activity and product selectivity. Improving porosity of those catalysts would be a promising strategy to enhance the performance of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ conversion. Obviously, MCOFs could fulfil the prerequisites and hold high promise in addressing these challenges. In particular, 2D Co-COFs containing Coporphyrin moieties have played a major role in the application of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ -to-CO reduction. For example, the 2D Co-COFs displayed high Faradaic efficiency (FECO, $90\%)$ ) and a turnover number (TON) of 1352 for aqueous electrochemical reduction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to CO, indicating high selectivity over competing proton reduction in this system (Figure 12a).[37] In addition, the organic strut units could influence the performance of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ electroreduction by systematically tuning the different electron withdrawing groups. In particular, Co-COF-F has been demonstrated to have a better performance than Co-COF, Co-COF-(OMe)2, and ${\mathsf{C o-C O F-F_{4}}}$ (Figure 12b).[53] Furthermore, the electron mobility of the organic struts in this system is also crucial for the performance. A 2D Co
COF consisting of a higher electron donor TTF units can serve as electrocatalysts for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ -to-CO conversion with high $F E_{C O}$ $(91.3\%$ , $-0.7\vee$ and possess high cycling stability $(>40\mathsf{h})$ .[38] Significantly, the maximum $F E_{00}$ can reach up to almost $100\%$ $(99.7\%$ , -0.8 V) by using its as-exfoliated ultrathin 2D nanosheets (Figure 12d). The possible reasons point to that those TTF units can facilitate the valence transition from $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{I I})$ to $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{I})$ with highly efficient, thereby promoting the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ -to-CO conversion (Figure 12e).
Mechanistic understanding of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction using MCOF materials is essential. Huang group demonstrated the 2D ReCOF are capable of promoting the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ -to-CO conversion under visible light illumination with high selectivity $(98\%)$ and better activity than its homogeneous (Figure 12f).[43] Transient absorption (TA) and $\mathsf{X}$ -ray transient absorption (XTA) spectroscopy indicated that this system could undergo facile intramolecular charge transfer through electron transfer from photoexcited COF to Re moieties (Figure 12g). Moreover, experimental data supported that three key intermediate species that are responsible for the efficient charge separation, induction period, and rate limiting step in $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction for mechanistic pathway (Figure 12h).
## 3.2. Molecule adsorption and separation
Because of the excellent chemical stability of MCOFs, they can be potentially used as solid adsorbents for molecular absorption. However, the available internal space of MCOFs is crucial for their absorption behavior. For example, the non-interpenetrated CuCOF exhibited a continuous adsorption of methyl orange, while only a negligible amount of adsorption was observed using doubly-interpenetrated Cu-COF (Figure 13a).[50] As increasing of the dye size, a similar amount of hydroxynaphthol blue was only adsorbed by non-interpenetrated Cu-COF (Figure 13b). However, for the largest size methyl blue, both Cu-COFs did not show any major uptake (Figure 13c), highlighting the importance of pore sizes in MCOFs for adaptive guest inclusion.
Such pore-size effect of MCOFs is also important for the molecular separation. It is generally accepted that wide pores are not beneficial for the capturing substrates and size-selective molecular sieving, especially separating some challenging isomer mixtures. Therefore, the development of 3D MCOFs containing micropores would be a promising way to address this issue. One of efficient strategies is to enhance the structural interpenetration in 3D MCOFs. For example, our group realized a 3D Zn-COF that adopt a 7-fold interpenetrated open framework with around $7.8\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ wide tubular channels (Figure 13d).[48] Significantly, this microporous Zn-COF can be used as stationary phases for high performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) to separate xylene isomers (Figure 13e). The high preference for o-xylene can be attributed to the host-guest interactions between its methyl groups and the polar salen groups of the frameworks, giving rise to greater retention on the Zn-COF column. This work further indicates that the suitable pore size of MCOFs is one of key factors for molecular separation.
## 3.3. Optics and Sensing
Although many COFs have luminescent behaviour and thus can be used for chemical sensing, two problems would restrict them for further applications: (i) They generally have single emission peaks with a broad distribution, which hinders ratiometric applications such as luminescent thermometers. (ii) Their emission peaks fall in the range from 400 to $600~\mathsf{n m}$ which is not suitable for biological applications. The introduction of trivalent lanthanide ions that usually exhibit multiple sharp emission peaks and NIR emission may expand the luminescent properties. For example, Carlos group recently reported that both Tb-Eu-COF and Dy-COF can be successfully used as luminescent thermometers in the $10-360\mathsf{K}$ and $280-440~\mathsf{K}$ ranges (Figure 14a-b), respectively, exhibiting excellent sensing properties with thermal sensitivity up to $1.403\%$ $\mathsf{K}^{-1}$ .[44] This work demonstrates the first example of Ln-COFs for luminescent thermometry.
The trace detection of toxic gas molecules at the parts-perbillion (ppb) level is required in our daily life but remains a great challenge. 2D MCOFs with highly intrinsic conductivity and structural stability could be used as chemiresistive senors for ppb level-based gas detection. For example, the 2D Ni-COFs have been demonstrated to possess a high conductivity of $2.51\times10^{-3}$ $\mathsf{s}/\mathsf{m}$ , which promotes the integration into chemiresistive devices for gas sensing with ppb limits of detection, such as 70 ppb for $N H_{3}$ , 204 ppb for ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}$ , 5 ppb for NO, and 16 ppb for ${\mathsf{N O}}_{2}$ (Figure 15).[42] Note that these values are comparable or superior to the most sensitive sensors. Such ultrahigh sensitivities are attributed to the charge transfer interactions between the analytes and Niphthalocyanine component of the framework. This work provides a outstanding case of conductive MCOFs for chemiresistive device application.
## 4. Conclusions and Future Perspectives
This Minireview describes the development of 2D and 3D MCOFs from the design and synthesis to their emerging applications. Several building blocks are employed to synthesize MCOFs with the functionalities of catalysis, gas adsorption, and sensing etc. Despite these meritorious examples, the development of MCOFs is still in its infancy and we want to encourage more research into this direction.
Based on our long-term understanding of MOFs, COFs, and supramolecular coordination cages, many improvements of MCOFs are still desirable. First, in view of the current interest in catalysis using MCOFs, the continued development of mixed metal-covalent organic frameworks (MMCOFs) will be of great interest to explore the cooperative effect. In particular, compared with homometallic MCOFs, heterometallic MMCOFs bring about the opportunity to take advantage of the complexity and the synergism derived from the presence of different metals, which would give rise to multi-functional heterogeneous catalysts capable of remarkable catalytic performance. On a fundamental level, a major issue is the determination of precise position for those metals in the structures, which is beneficial to understand the metal-metal cooperative effect and the metal-property relationships for the catalytic performance. Achieving the X-ray crystal structures or employing advanced high-resolution $(<2.0$ Å) TEM for MCOFs that can be expected to solve these challenges. Second, considering the progress in designing spatially segregated environments of metal ions within 3D frameworks, further development of 3D chiral MCOFs is certainly necessary for the applications of enantioselective catalysis and sensing, and enantiomer sieving. Moreover, fundamental understanding of the delivery, transmission, and amplification of chirality from the molecules to assembled frameworks is crucial for the development of chiral MCOFs. Third, because of the diverse structures of metal clusters, the functionalized metal clusters are very promising building blocks to construct MCOFs, However, the main challenge is to overcome the compatibility, stability, and solubility of those clusters during the COF synthesis. Novel MCOF materials are expected to explore some new field in magnetic, electronic, transport and optical properties. Fourth, considering the advantages of MCOFs with enhanced gas adsorption, the further development of ultrathin 2D MCOF membranes with high selectivity and permeability is of great importance on industry applications. On the application level, a remaining challenge lies in the preparation of defect-free and large area 2D MCOF nanosheets. The introduction of metals in the COF could be potentially enhance the crystallinity and strength the layer stacking during layer-to-layer assembly of the 2D MCOF nanosheets. Furthermore, the integration of chemical reactions and membrane-based separations in the same MCOFs device would be a promising direction for the development of membrane reactors in further.
In summary, the field of MCOFs will continue to be an active and challenging playground. It is hoped that this Minireview will help the design, synthesis and state-of-the-art application of MCOFs, and inspire future developments.
